.²1 this particle is most generally used to report the very words spoken
 or supposed to be spoken by some one, as represented by quotation marks
 in <lang>English</lang>. The speech reported may be (1) a single word used merely to
 express what the form of the word is, when it is used as it is ({#SabdasvarUpadyotaka#});
 {#kUjantaM rAmarAmeti maDuraM maDurAkzaram#} <ls>Rāmarakṣā.</ls> {#ata eva gavityAha#}
 <ls>Bh.</ls>; (2) or a substantive, which must be put in the nominative case
 when its meaning is to be indicated

 ({#prAtipadikArTadyotaka#}); {#cayastvizAmityavaDAritaM purA … kamAdamuM#}
 {#nArada ityaboDi saH#} <ls>Śi. 1</ls>, <ls n="Śi.">3</ls>; {#avEmi cEnAmanaGeti#} <ls>R. 14. 40</ls>; {#dilIpa#}
 {#iti rAjenduH#} <ls>R. 1. 12</ls>; sometimes with <ab>acc.</ab> {#kEvartamiti yaM prAhuH#}
 <ls>Ms. 10. 34</ls>; <ls>Bg. 6. 2</ls>; (3) or a whole sentence when {#iti#} is merely
 used at the end of that sentence; ({#vAkyArTadyotaka#}); {#jYAsyasi kiyad#}
 {#Bujo me rakzati mOrvIMkiRANka iti#} <ls>Śi. 1. 13</ls>; {#tayormunikumArayoranyataraH#}
 {#kaTayati akzamAlAmupayAcitumAgatosmIti#} <ls>K. 151</ls>.
.²2 Besides this general sense {#iti#} has the following senses:--
.³({%a%}) Cause, as expressed by ‘because’, ‘since’, ‘on the ground that’,
 in <lang>English</lang>; {#vEdeSiko'smIti pfcCAmi#} <ls>U.</ls>; {#purARamityeva naHsADu sarvam#}
 <ls>M. 1. 2</ls>, <ab>oft.</ab> with {#kim#} <ab>q. v.</ab>
.³({%b%}) Purpose or motive, as expressed by ‘that’, ‘in order that’ {#SarIrasya#}
 {#vinASo mA BUditi mayedamutkzipya samAnItam#} <ls>K. 320</ls>; <ls>R. 1. 37</ls>.
.³({%c%}) Thus, to mark the conclusion (<ab>opp.</ab> {#aTa#}); {#iti praTamo'NkaH#}
 thus or here ends the first Act.
.³({%d%}) It is often used to include under one head a number of separate
 objects grouped together; {#pfTivyApastejo vAyurAkASaM kAlo digAtmA mana#}
 {#iti dravyARi#} <ls>T. S.</ls>
.³({%e%}) So, thus, in this manner; {#ityuktavantaM pariraBya dorByAm#} <ls>Ki. 11. 80</ls>.
.³({%f%}) Of this nature or description; {#gOraSvaH puruzo hastIti jAtiH#}.
.³({%g%}) As follows, to the following effect; {#rAmABiDAno harirityuvAca#} <ls>R. 13. 1</ls>.
.³({%h%}) As for, in the capacity of, as regards, showing capacity or relation;
 {#piteti sa pUjyaH, aDyApaka iti nindyaH, SIGramiti sukaraM, niBftamiti#}
 {#cintanIyaM Bavet#} <ls>Ś. 3</ls>.
.³({%i%}) It is often used with the name of an author to form an {%Avyayibhāva%}
 <ab>comp.</ab> {#itipARini#} thus according to Pāṇini.
.³({%j%}) Illustration (usually with {#Adi#}); {#indurinduriva SrImAnityAdO#}
 {#tadananvayaH#} <ls>Chandr.</ls>; {#gOH SuklaScalo qitTa ityAdO#} <ls>K. P. 2</ls>.
.³({%k%}) A quotation or an opinion accepted; {#iti pARiniH, ityApiSaliH#},
 {#ityamaraH, viSvaH#} <ab>&c.</ab>
.³({%l%}) It is often used by commentators after quoting a rule in the
 sense of ‘according to such a rule’; {#Saki liN ca#} (<ls>P. III. 3. 172</ls>) {#iti#}
 {#SakyArTe liN#} <ls>Malli.</ls> Other senses mentioned are:--
.³({%m%}) Manifestation.
.³({%n%}) Order.
.³({%o%}) Arrangement.
.³({%p%}) Identity.
.³({%q%}) Proximity.
.³({%r%}) Visibility.
.³({%s%}) Excess or superiority.
.³({%t%}) Requiring. ({#iti svarUpe sAnniDye vivakzAniyame mate . hetO#}
 {#prakArapratyakzaprakASezyavaDAraRe, evamarTe samAptO ca ..#} <ls>Hem.</ls>).

